+### Native Agent Hub children (`ctx.agents`)
+
+Interactive host extensions can use `ctx.agents` to manage native task children without an LLM dispatch step. It is undefined in print, JSON, RPC, subagent, and collab guest contexts.
+
+- `list()` returns owned descendant task jobs with native job/agent status, cwd, model, last activity and optional live `sessionId`.
+- `spawn({ agent, task, name?, model?, cwd?, initialization? })` returns a managed job promptly through the enabled native task tool. Normal spawn policy, async settings, concurrency, model grants and lifecycle still apply. Operator-origin spawn and send results remain in the native job/Hub views and do not wake the parent model; ordinary model-origin task completion delivery is unchanged. Blocking agent types and disabled async jobs are rejected. A fresh persistent parent journal is materialized with the native `ensureOnDisk`/`flush` path, so spawning can be the first command without a chat turn; `--no-session` remains unsupported. Optional `cwd` asserts that the source workspace matches the parent; cross-workspace continuations are rejected.
+- `focus(handle)`, `interrupt(handle)` and `cancel(handle)` use the native focus, abort and lifecycle paths. Cancel preserves the child's transcript as a killed agent.
+- `send(handle, message, { streamingBehavior? })` returns a managed follow-up job. It awaits native revival and rechecks the selected handle before admitting the job, so stale selections reject without submitting input. The model turn completes asynchronously; a failed job exposes its native `error`. Its prompt uses the native Hub delivery path; streaming behavior is `steer` by default or `followUp`.
+
+Keep the full returned handle: `agentId`, `jobId`, `jobStartedAt`, `runStartedAt`, `sessionFile`, `generation`, and optional `sessionId`. Refresh with `list()` after a queued child starts, after sending, or after any turn changes. Old job rows remain readable; mutations reject stale root sessions, newer runs/jobs, replaced child generations and foreign roots.
+
+`initialization` is an array of trusted extension-authored `{ customType, data }` custom entries. These are persisted and flushed before the child's extension `session_start` handlers run, and before its first prompt. This field is not accepted by the LLM-facing task parameter schema. A child extension can read its entry from `ctx.sessionManager.getBranch()` during `session_start` and await its provider-specific bootstrap with the child's actual `ctx.sessionManager.getSessionId()`. Initialization metadata is not replayed as conversation text. Bootstrap extensions must reject provider turns until their own required initialization has succeeded.
+
 ## 3) Command context (`ExtensionCommandContext`)
 
 Command handlers additionally get:
